 On a Curiosity Nano Base for Click boards (AC164162), ``mb1=``, ``mb2=``
 and ``mb3=`` plug a Click board into mikroBUS socket 1-3: SPI on SPI0, I2C
-on TWI0 (PA2 SDA, PA3 SCL; host mode, ``avr8x-twi``). Every Click of the
-shared catalog works here: ``thermo8``, ``accel``, ``buttong``/``r``/``y``,
-``7seg``, ``relay``, ``8x8g``/``r``/``y``/``b``, ``buzz``, ``oledc``,
-``thumbstick`` and ``rtc6`` (see the PIC32CM MC00 Curiosity Pro for what
-each one models). The Click is ``/machine/mbN-click``. A UART Click on
-socket 1 or 3 takes their shared USART2 (PF4/PF5, the second
-``-serial``). AN is not wired yet, so the ``pot`` Click is refused here.
+on TWI0 (PA2 SDA, PA3 SCL; host mode, ``avr8x-twi``), AN on PD4, PD5 and
+PD7 (ADC0 AIN4, AIN5, AIN7; PD7 is also VREFA). Every Click of the shared
+catalog works here (see the PIC32CM MC00 Curiosity Pro for what each one
+models). The Click is ``/machine/mbN-click``. A UART Click on socket 1 or
+3 takes their shared USART2 (PF4/PF5, the second ``-serial``). Socket 2's
+UART is USART1 (PC0/PC1), the virtual COM port's lines: a UART Click there
+shares them with ``-serial`` 0 (``/chardevs/mb2-share``), so the console
+sees what the MCU sends the Click, and what either sends reaches the MCU
+(on the board, both at once would collide). USART1 then runs at the
+Click's baud rate, the console's too.
 
 Firmware comes from ``-kernel`` or ``-bios``::
 
